  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do turbine blades catch the wind?",
      "a": "The blades are shaped like aircraft wings. Wind flowing over them creates lift, which pulls each blade sideways and round, so the tips move much faster than the wind itself.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are wind turbine blades twisted?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does blade pitch do?",
      "a": "Pitch is the angle of a blade to the wind. Motors adjust it to get the most power in gentle wind, keep the power steady in strong wind, and turn the blades edge-on to stop the rotor in a storm.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens to a wind turbine in a storm?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a wind turbine need a gearbox?",
      "a": "The rotor turns slowly, but a standard generator needs to spin fast to make electricity at the right frequency. The gearbox raises the speed about 100 times, like riding a bike in its highest gear.",
      "asks": [
        "Do all wind turbines have gearboxes?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a turbine turn to face the wind?",
      "a": "A wind vane on the nacelle senses the wind direction, and a computer drives yaw motors that rotate the nacelle on a big toothed ring at the top of the tower.",
      "asks": [
        "What is yaw on a wind turbine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a direct-drive wind turbine?",
      "a": "It has no gearbox: the rotor turns a very large, slow generator with many magnets around its rim. Fewer moving parts mean less to break, which matters for turbines far out at sea.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a gearless wind turbine?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Many large wind turbines turn roughly 10 to 20 times a minute, with a gearbox raising the speed for the generator.",
    "Direct-drive wind turbines use large permanent-magnet generators and have no gearbox."
  ],
